    <div class="prose text-themed">

      <h3>3.1 Self-Tuning Retrieval</h3>
      <p>
        Existing agent memory systems expose a single retrieval interface: embed a query, return the <em>k</em> nearest vectors. HEBBS instead exposes retrieval as a <em>parameter space</em> that the agent navigates per query:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Strategy selection:</strong> similarity, temporal, causal, or analogical.</li>
        <li><strong>Scoring weights:</strong> a four-dimensional blend of relevance, recency, importance, and reinforcement.</li>
        <li><strong>Strategy-specific parameters:</strong> HNSW beam width (<code>ef_search</code>), causal traversal depth, analogical <em>alpha</em> (structural vs. embedding blend), result count <em>k</em>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This creates a combinatorial space of retrieval configurations. The key insight is that the agent, not the engine, is best positioned to navigate this space, because the agent understands the query intent that determines optimal retrieval.
      </p>

      <h4>The eval-tune-store loop</h4>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Eval generation.</strong> The agent reads the indexed content (via <code>hebbs list</code>) and generates domain-specific evaluation queries with expected keywords or facts that should appear in results.</li>
        <li><strong>Baseline measurement.</strong> The agent runs each eval query with default retrieval settings and scores keyword recall against expectations.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure diagnosis.</strong> The agent analyzes which queries underperformed and why: insufficient <em>k</em>, wrong strategy, missing entity names in cues, suboptimal weight blend.</li>
        <li><strong>Tuned re-run.</strong> The agent re-runs failed queries with adjusted parameters and measures improvement.</li>
        <li><strong>Strategy storage.</strong> The agent stores learned retrieval strategies back into HEBBS as high-importance memories: "For temporal queries, use weights 0.3:0.5:0.2:0 with k=10."</li>
        <li><strong>Strategy recall.</strong> In subsequent conversations, the agent recalls its own retrieval strategies before executing queries, applying domain-tuned parameters automatically.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This loop has three properties that distinguish it from static retrieval optimization. First, it is <em>domain-specific</em>: a legal agent discovers different optimal strategies than a sales agent or a coding agent operating on different vaults. Second, it is <em>agent-driven</em>: the agent generates better evals than any built-in heuristic because it understands the domain semantics. Third, it is <em>self-reinforcing</em>: stored strategies are themselves memories subject to decay and reinforcement. Strategies that are recalled frequently (because they work) strengthen, while unused strategies fade.
      </p>

      <h3>3.2 Contradiction Detection and Resolution</h3>
      <p>
        When a new memory is ingested, HEBBS searches for potential conflicts among existing memories using HNSW approximate nearest-neighbor search (<em>k</em>=10 neighbors, similarity threshold &ge; 0.5). Each candidate pair is evaluated through an LLM-based structured classifier.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>LLM classifier.</strong> The candidate pair is sent to an LLM for structured classification into one of three categories:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Contradiction:</strong> The two memories make conflicting factual claims that cannot both be true (e.g., "the project uses PostgreSQL" vs. "the project uses MongoDB").</li>
        <li><strong>Revision:</strong> The newer memory supersedes the older one. The same fact has been updated (e.g., "the API uses token auth" followed by "we switched the API to OAuth2").</li>
        <li><strong>Dismiss:</strong> The memories are compatible, unrelated, or complementary despite surface similarity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The classifier returns a confidence score alongside its verdict, enabling downstream filtering by confidence threshold.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Two-phase commit.</strong> Detection and resolution are decoupled into two phases:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Detect:</strong> The LLM classifier produces a <code>PendingContradiction</code> record stored in a dedicated column family, containing both memory IDs, content snippets, confidence score, and the classifier's verdict.</li>
        <li><strong>Commit:</strong> An agent reviews pending contradictions and issues final verdicts. Contradictions create bidirectional <code>CONTRADICTS</code> edges. Revisions create unidirectional <code>REVISED_FROM</code> edges. Dismissals delete the pending record. All graph mutations are applied atomically via batch write.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This two-phase design decouples the detection path from the resolution path, allowing asynchronous processing without blocking ingestion. It also preserves agent authority over conflict resolution: the LLM proposes, but the agent (or a human operator) decides. This mirrors the neuroscience model: the ACC detects conflict automatically, but the prefrontal cortex deliberates and resolves it.
      </p>

      <h3>3.3 Hebbian Associative Embeddings</h3>
      <p>
        Standard vector memory systems assign each memory a single embedding (its content vector) and retrieve by cosine similarity. This supports one question: "what looks like this?" But agents need temporal reasoning ("what happened next?"), causal reasoning ("what caused this?"), and analogical reasoning ("what is structurally similar in another domain?").
      </p>
      <p>
        HEBBS addresses this by maintaining two embeddings per memory: a static <em>content embedding</em> and an evolving <em>associative embedding</em>, initialized to the content embedding and updated via Hebbian learning as relational edges are created.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Per-edge-type offset vectors.</strong> For each edge type (CausedBy, FollowedBy, RelatedTo, etc.), HEBBS maintains a learned offset vector of the same dimensionality as the embeddings. When an edge of type &tau; from memory <em>i</em> to memory <em>j</em> is created, the offset is updated:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-themed-surface rounded-lg p-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto">
        &delta;<sub>&tau;</sub> &larr; normalize(&delta;<sub>&tau;</sub> + &eta; &middot; (e<sub>a</sub><sup>(j)</sup> &minus; e<sub>a</sub><sup>(i)</sup>))
      </div>
      <p>
        where &eta; = 0.1 is the learning rate. Over time, &delta;<sub>&tau;</sub> learns the typical <em>direction</em> in embedding space that a &tau;-typed edge traverses. This enables three novel retrieval operations:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Forward causal traversal.</strong> To find what a seed memory <em>s</em> typically causes: q = normalize(e<sub>a</sub><sup>(s)</sup> + &delta;<sub>CausedBy</sub>)</li>
        <li><strong>Backward causal traversal.</strong> To find what typically causes a seed memory <em>s</em>: q = normalize(e<sub>a</sub><sup>(s)</sup> &minus; &delta;<sub>CausedBy</sub>)</li>
        <li><strong>Analogical reasoning.</strong> Given memories A, B, C, to find D such that A:B::C:D: q = normalize(e<sub>a</sub><sup>(C)</sup> + normalize(e<sub>a</sub><sup>(B)</sup> &minus; e<sub>a</sub><sup>(A)</sup>))</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is conceptually similar to word2vec's relational arithmetic but operates over <em>evolving</em> associative embeddings rather than static word vectors, and the offsets are learned incrementally per edge type rather than emergent from co-occurrence statistics.
      </p>

      <h3>3.4 Fast Encoding (Vault Ingestion)</h3>
      <p>
        Analogous to hippocampal fast encoding, HEBBS ingests memories with minimal latency. Markdown files are parsed into sections by heading hierarchy, each section producing a memory with content, heading path (as structured context), and an importance score. An LLM extracts atomic propositions from each section, creating fine-grained memories alongside the coarser section-level ones. Embeddings are computed locally via ONNX Runtime with no API calls. A file-watching daemon detects changes in real time, computing SHA-256 checksums to skip unchanged sections.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each memory is stored in RocksDB across multiple column families: default (content + metadata), temporal (B-tree index by entity and timestamp), vectors (HNSW index), graph (typed edges with bidirectional key encoding), and associative (evolving Hebbian embeddings).
      </p>

      <h3>3.5 File-First Architecture: Portable Cognition</h3>
      <p>
        HEBBS separates knowledge into two planes: a <em>content plane</em> (the user's markdown files) and a <em>cognition plane</em> (the <code>.hebbs/</code> directory). The content plane is the source of truth: human-readable, version-controlled, and unchanged by the engine. The cognition plane contains all derived state: embeddings, graph edges, temporal indices, the manifest, and cached decay scores. It is entirely rebuildable from the content plane via <code>hebbs init</code> and <code>hebbs index</code>.
      </p>
      <p>This separation yields three properties:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Portability.</strong> The <code>.hebbs/</code> directory is a self-contained cognition artifact. It can be copied to another machine, shared across a team, or handed to a different agent. Recipients get the full memory graph (insights, contradictions, associative embeddings, and learned retrieval strategies) without re-indexing.</li>
        <li><strong>Rebuild guarantee.</strong> Deleting <code>.hebbs/</code> and re-running initialization reconstructs the entire cognition layer from source files. This provides crash safety without transaction logs and simplifies migration across engine versions.</li>
        <li><strong>Selective indexing.</strong> A <code>.hebbsignore</code> file (using <code>.gitignore</code> syntax) controls which files enter the cognition plane. Private files, drafts, and generated content are excluded at the boundary. The daemon re-reads this file on each config reload cycle with no restart required.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This file-first design treats memory as a file-system citizen: inspectable, portable, and composable with existing developer workflows (git, rsync, CI pipelines). The content plane can be versioned independently of the cognition plane, enabling reproducible memory states for benchmarking and auditing.
      </p>

      <h3>3.6 Four Recall Strategies</h3>
      <p>The agent explicitly selects a recall strategy based on query intent:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Similarity:</strong> Embed the cue, query HNSW. Complexity: O(log n &middot; ef_search).</li>
        <li><strong>Temporal:</strong> Range query over B-tree index by entity and time window. Complexity: O(log n + k).</li>
        <li><strong>Causal:</strong> Graph traversal from a seed memory along typed edges (forward, backward, or both) up to depth 10. Complexity: O(b<sup>d</sup>) where b is branching factor and d is depth.</li>
        <li><strong>Analogical:</strong> Dual HNSW search with wider beam (2x ef_search), scored by a blend of embedding similarity (&alpha;) and structural similarity (1 &minus; &alpha;), where structural similarity considers context key overlap (weight 0.4), value type matching (0.3), memory kind matching (0.2), and entity pattern matching (0.1). Default &alpha; = 0.5.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>All strategies share a composite scoring function:</p>
      <div class="bg-themed-surface rounded-lg p-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto">
        score = w<sub>r</sub> &middot; relevance + w<sub>t</sub> &middot; recency + w<sub>i</sub> &middot; importance + w<sub>f</sub> &middot; reinforcement
      </div>
      <p>
        where recency = max(0, 1 &minus; age/max_age), reinforcement = log<sub>2</sub>(1 + min(access_count, 100)) / log<sub>2</sub>(101), and default weights are w<sub>r</sub>=0.5, w<sub>t</sub>=0.2, w<sub>i</sub>=0.2, w<sub>f</sub>=0.1.
      </p>

      <h3>3.7 Consolidation (Reflection)</h3>
      <p>
        Analogous to sleep-dependent memory consolidation, HEBBS periodically clusters episodic memories and proposes <em>insights</em>: higher-level semantic summaries with lineage edges back to source episodes. The pipeline has four stages:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Cluster:</strong> K-means on associative embeddings (preferred) or content embeddings, with configurable minimum cluster size (default 3) and maximum clusters (default 50).</li>
        <li><strong>Propose:</strong> An LLM generates candidate insights per cluster.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate:</strong> A separate LLM pass validates candidates against source memories and existing insights to prevent redundancy.</li>
        <li><strong>Store:</strong> Validated insights are stored as Insight-kind memories with InsightFrom edges to source episodes.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Insights participate in all subsequent operations: they can be recalled, can themselves be consolidated, and are subject to the same decay process as episodes.
      </p>

      <h3>3.8 Adaptive Decay</h3>
      <p>
        Memories that are not accessed decay in importance following the Ebbinghaus forgetting curve:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-themed-surface rounded-lg p-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto">
        d(m) = importance(m) &middot; 2<sup>&minus;age(m)/h</sup> &middot; (1 + log<sub>2</sub>(1 + min(a, c)) / log<sub>2</sub>(1 + c))
      </div>
      <p>
        where <em>h</em> is the half-life (default 30 days), age(m) = now &minus; last_accessed(m) (reinforcement resets the clock), <em>a</em> is the access count, and <em>c</em> = 100 is the reinforcement cap.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reinforcement multiplier ranges from 1.0 (never accessed) to approximately 2.0 (at cap), preventing runaway scores while ensuring that frequently accessed memories live longer. A background sweep thread processes memories in batches of 10,000 (up to 1M per sweep), marking memories with d(m) &lt; 0.01 as auto-forget candidates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Critically, age is measured from <code>last_accessed_at</code>, not <code>created_at</code>. Every recall hit resets the decay clock: a direct computational analogue of memory reconsolidation, where retrieval restabilizes and extends a memory's lifetime.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>4. Experiments</h2>

      <h3>4.1 Agent-Driven Retrieval Tuning</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Setup.</strong> We indexed a vault of 52 markdown files from an enterprise legal department (contracts, board minutes, vendor assessments, compliance policies, risk registers, incident reports) using HEBBS with GPT-4o-mini as the LLM backend. Indexing produced 949 memories: 465 section-level memories and 484 LLM-extracted atomic propositions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Eval generation.</strong> A Claude Opus 4.6 agent read the vault contents via <code>hebbs list</code> and generated 20 evaluation queries spanning five query types: factual lookup (similarity), temporal evolution, causal reasoning, cross-entity pattern matching (analogical), and contradiction detection. Each query included expected keywords that should appear in results. The distribution was: 10 similarity, 3 temporal, 2 causal, 3 analogical, 2 contradiction.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Baseline.</strong> All 20 queries were run with default settings: <code>--strategy similarity</code>, <em>k</em>=5. The agent scored keyword recall against expected keywords.
      </p>
