    <div class="prose text-themed">

      <hr />

      <h2>5. Discussion</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Limitations.</strong> The self-tuning loop depends on agent quality: a weaker agent generates weaker evals and diagnoses failures less accurately. Contradiction detection requires an LLM provider, adding latency and cost to the ingestion path. The two-phase commit design mitigates blocking, but detection quality is bounded by the LLM's reasoning fidelity. The Hebbian offset vectors assume that relational patterns are approximately linear in embedding space, an assumption inherited from word2vec that may not hold for all edge types. Consolidation quality also depends on LLM reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Testable predictions from the neuroscience mapping.</strong> The explicit brain-region mapping generates predictions beyond what we have tested: (1) interleaved consolidation of old and new memories should produce better knowledge integration than batch processing (predicted by complementary learning systems theory), (2) decay half-life should be tunable per memory kind, with propositions decaying faster than insights (predicted by memory transformation theory), and (3) associative embeddings should cluster memories by <em>relational role</em> rather than content similarity over time.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Future work.</strong> The file-first architecture makes multi-agent shared memory concrete: multiple agents can share a single <code>.hebbs/</code> directory, with conflict resolution mediated by the <code>device_id</code> and <code>logical_clock</code> fields already present in the memory schema. Cross-vault consolidation, where an agent operating across multiple projects transfers insights between domains, would test the limits of analogical recall. The self-tuning loop could be extended with automated strategy selection, where the agent learns to predict the optimal strategy from query features without running evals.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>6. Conclusion</h2>

      <p>
        Agent memory has two problems: the memory degrades, and the retrieval never improves. Current systems address neither.
      </p>
      <p>
        HEBBS addresses both. Six neuroscience-grounded mechanisms maintain the memory store: contradiction detection, Hebbian associative learning, consolidation, and adaptive decay prevent the degradation that accumulates over weeks of operation. Self-tuning retrieval addresses the second problem: agents evaluate their own recall, tune parameters per domain, and store strategies as memories that persist and strengthen through use. In a 52-document legal vault, agent-driven tuning improved keyword recall from 59% to 88% in a single pass.
      </p>
      <p>
        The system compiles to a single Rust binary with sub-10ms recall at 10M memories, a portable <code>.hebbs/</code> cognition layer, and <code>.hebbsignore</code> privacy control. Contradiction detection and Hebbian associative embeddings are, to our knowledge, novel contributions to the agent memory literature. Self-tuning retrieval, where the memory engine learns how to be used, is a new capability.
      </p>
      <p>
        We release HEBBS as open-source software and invite the community to evaluate whether self-tuning, neuroscience-grounded memory produces the same benefits for AI agents that evolution produced for biological ones.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Implementation Details</h2>
      <p>HEBBS is implemented in Rust using the following stack:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> RocksDB with six column families (Default, Temporal, Vectors, Graph, VectorsAssociative, Meta, Pending).</li>
        <li><strong>Embeddings:</strong> ONNX Runtime with EmbeddingGemma-300M (768 dimensions). All embeddings computed locally.</li>
        <li><strong>Serialization:</strong> Bitcode format with gamma encoding, approximately 20-30% smaller than bincode. Optional CRC-32C checksums for integrity.</li>
        <li><strong>Indexing:</strong> HNSW for vector similarity, B-tree (via RocksDB sorted iteration) for temporal queries, bidirectional key encoding for graph traversal.</li>
        <li><strong>Concurrency:</strong> Tokio async runtime for I/O-bound operations. Dedicated OS thread (not Tokio) for CPU-bound decay sweeps to avoid async starvation.</li>
        <li><strong>IDs:</strong> ULID (128-bit, time-sortable, globally unique).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Memory record size:</strong> ~200-220 bytes without embedding, +3,072 bytes with 768-dimensional embedding.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Graph edge encoding:</strong> Forward keys are prefixed <code>0xF0</code>, reverse keys <code>0xF1</code>, enabling efficient bidirectional traversal via prefix scan. Edge metadata is 12 bytes: confidence (f32 LE) + timestamp (u64 LE).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Bounded operations:</strong> All loops, traversal depths, and batch sizes are explicitly bounded. MAX_TOP_K = 1,000, MAX_TRAVERSAL_DEPTH = 10, MAX_PRIME_MEMORIES = 200, MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH = 64KB.
      </p>

